<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.11.2: The Factored Form of a Quadratic Expression and the X-Intercepts of Its Graph</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01924</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.11.2: The Factored Form of a Quadratic Expression and the X-Intercepts of Its Graph</md:title>
    <md:uuid>31dc3a40-452d-45c0-becb-d26ba759d181</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Consider two functions defined by \(f(x)=x(x+4)\) and \(g(x)=x(x-4)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Consider the function defined by \(f(x)=x(x+4)\).</item>
</list>
<para>Then, answer questions a–g, and create a table of values in which to enter the given x values and their outputs using the function.</para>

<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="fbf538bb-f2c6-4352-9d03-9245f6291f4a"><problem><para>
      <para> When the value of \(x=-4\), what is the value of \(f(x)\)? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:<emphasis/></para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>0</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is 0.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Now, answer questions a–d to analyze the function defined by \(f(x)=x(x+4)\).</item>
</list>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The \(x\)-intercepts for function \(f\) are \((-4,0)\) and \((0,0)\). These points have an \(x\)-coordinate that makes the output value 0.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="cd4fd094-81f0-44ce-bdc2-7c8ffd4a284c"><problem><para>
      <para> What is the value of the \(x\)-coordinate in the vertex of the graph?</para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:<emphasis/></para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>-2</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is -2.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the vertex of the graph of function \(f\)? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> The vertex appears to be at \((-2,-4)\). From the tables, the function values seem to change from decreasing to increasing at those points.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Consider the function defined by \(g(x)=x(x-4)\).
    
    Then, answer questions a–g, and create a table of values in which to enter the given x values and their outputs using the function.
  </item>
</list>

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="e2b10c58-e3be-4468-b659-41230961de8d"><problem><para>
      <para> When the value of \(x=-4\), what is the value of \(g(x)\)? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:<emphasis/></para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>32</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is 32.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Now, answer questions a–d to analyze the function defined by \(g(x)=x(x-4)\).</item>
</list>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The \(x\)-intercepts for function \(g\) are \((0,0)\) and \((4,0)\). These points have an \(x\)-coordinate that makes the output value 0.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="3192dc06-76cb-40d2-9e9b-1ead9c311094"><problem><para>
      <para> What is the value of the \(x\)-coordinate in the vertex of the graph? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:<emphasis/></para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>2</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is 2.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the vertex of the graph of function \(g\)? Be prepared to show your reasoning. </para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> The vertex appears to be at \((2,-4)\). From the tables, the function values seem to change from decreasing to increasing at those points.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course.</item>
</list>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Plot the points from the tables on the same coordinate plane. (Consider using different colors or markings for each set of points so you can tell them apart.) </para>
      <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Coordinate grid with two graphs shown by plotted points."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1817dcc93434ecbd070c0706316edaae60d639c3"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Make a couple of observations about how the two graphs compare. </para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <list>
      <item> The \(x\)-intercept of both graphs is at \((0,0)\). </item>
      <item> The graphs seem to be a reflection of each other across the \(y\)-axis. </item>
      <item> The vertices both have a \(y\)-coordinate of -4 and also mirror one another across the \(y\)-axis. </item>
      <item> Each function mirrors itself across a vertical line through its vertex. </item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
